**Protein Subcellular Localization Prediction Project Report**

Introduction
=============================
Protein subcellular localization is a critical step in understanding protein function and is instrumental in applications such as drug development and disease diagnosis. In this project, we focused on classifying Gram-positive bacterial proteins into one of four subcellular locations using various machine learning methods. We followed a complete machine learning pipeline, including feature extraction, model training, evaluation, and cross-validation.
=============================

Dataset and Preprocessing
For this project, we used the `g_data.csv` dataset. This dataset contains protein sequences from gram-negative bacteria and their associated subcellular locations. The dataset contains a total of 523 protein samples.
Each protein belongs to one of four classes:
Fold1 (Cell membrane)
Fold2 (Cell wall)
Fold3 (Cytoplasm)
Fold4 (Extracellular)

Feature Extraction
In order to extract features from the protein sequences, numerical representations of the protein sequences were created. A list of features in which we were able to extract:
Amino Acid Composition (AAC): Percentage frequency of each of the 20 amino acids.
Amino Acid Occurrence Counts: Raw counts of each amino acid in the sequence.
Sequence Length: Total number of amino acids in the sequence.

These 41 features (20 counts + 20 composition + 1 length) were then saved to separate CSV and ARFF file (‘features.csv’ and ‘features.arff’) for use with Python and cross examination/confirmation with Weka.

Classifier Training
The following machine learning classifiers were trained with the numerical data preprocessed in the previous section:
K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)
Support Vector Machine (SVM)
Naïve Bayes (NB)
Random Forest (RF)
Bagging
AdaBoost
Multi-layer Perceptron (MLP)

Each model was trained using a layered 80/20 train-test split; 80 percent of the data used to train the model, 20 percent of the remaining data used to test the model. Input features were standardized using `StandardScaler`.

Evaluation Metrics
Each classifier was evaluated on the test set using:
Accuracy: Proportion of correct predictions, true positive predictions.
Confusion Matrix: Count of true/false positives/negatives for each fold.
Classification Report: Precision, recall, and F1-score per class.
Sensitivity (Recall): True positive rate per class (fold).
Specificity: True negative rate per class (fold).
Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC): Balanced metric accounting for all confusion matrix elements.
5-Fold Cross-Validation Accuracy: Mean and standard deviation over 5 splits.

