Discussion and Analysis
Models performed well on classes with many samples. For example, Fold3 and Fold1 performed better than the remaining folds due to their larger sample sizes. Fold2 produced consistent yet underperforming results within each model. This is likely due to the smaller sample size of this class. As with most projects of this nature, including more training data could improve results for rare classes. The random forest and multi-layer perceptron (MLP) yielded the most balanced performance of the utilized models. A wider selection of features or an integration of more complex features could improve the overall performance of the models used.

Conclusion
We successfully implemented a machine learning pipeline for multiclass classification of Gram-positive bacterial proteins. Using amino acid composition and sequence properties as extracted features appeared to be effective. Of all the models used, the random forest and multi-layer perceptron models (MLP) were the top-performing classifiers. The evaluation of each of these models covered accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, MCC, and cross-validation. In using these evaluative measures, a comprehensive performance overview of each model has been provided. To continue the work started on projects such as this, one could test differing/more advanced biological features with other deep learning models.

